From 1b4a458a7fef363d5ae9d0e709b4e3fc637cc2fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kirn Gill II <segin2005@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 10 Jun 2026 23:03:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 64/98] libm: C23 narrowing / IEEE totalorder / reentrant +
 obsolete families
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds the remaining libm surface (math_narrowing.c, math_totalorder.c,
math_obsolete.c, math_reentrant.c, cmath_log10.c) with the public
declarations in <math.h>/<complex.h>, wired into the build, plus the
audit-found correctness fixes applied in place:

- totalorder(): apply the sign-magnitude key uniformly so negative NaNs
  order below finite values (IEEE 754-2019 total order); the prior
  ad-hoc NaN path mis-ordered them.
- C23 narrowing fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv: round ONCE to the narrow type via
  the x87 precision-control field (no operand pre-rounding / double
  rounding); ffma/dfma truly fuse via fmaf/fma.
- scalb/scalbf: correct infinite-exponent and sign-of-zero edges.
- lgammaf_r returns float, lgammal_r returns long double (were double).

Tests: tests/lib/m/unit/test_libm_new.c and property/prop_totalorder.c
(both green against host libm).

Known gap (deferred): the long-double (mathl.c) and long-double complex
variants still route through double, losing 80-bit precision and the
long-double exponent range — a separate effort.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
